{#-
  The type mapping of the Rust worked example, and the naming rules that go
  with it.

  FR-ENV-009 is why this is not a filter of `tpl`, and BR-ENV-002 is why it
  never will be: a type mapping is an opinion, and an opinion belongs to the
  project that holds it. FR-ENV-011 fixes the form the opinion takes — a
  template macro at this exact path — and FR-PROJ-017 has `tpl init` write a
  starter one here, which this file replaces outright. FR-EX-008 fixes the
  obligation: a Rust type for every value of `data_type` the model reports for
  a column of `sakila`, `world` and `freight`. There are 39 of them, and all 39
  are branched on below.

  Nothing falls through. The final branch calls `fail()`, per FR-ENV-020 and
  FR-SEM-014, so a type this file has never met ends the render with 65 and
  names the column, rather than reaching the Rust compiler as a plausible
  guess.

  The mapping reads `data_type` through the `sql_type` filter of FR-ENV-039,
  and the unsigned attribute through the `unsigned` part of FR-CTX-015, which
  FR-CTX-038 derives from `column_type` because no catalogue field carries it.

  Every mapping below was measured against the server of FR-EX-006: a real
  column of each of the 39 types was read through `sqlx` 0.9 and decoded into
  the type this file names. The types whose direction was in doubt — the two
  adapters, the temporal types, the two address types and the integer a BIT
  becomes — were bound back as query parameters as well. Where no type of the
  standard battery decoded a column — DECIMAL and the eight spatial types —
  the mapping names an adapter this example renders beside it, in
  `rust/schema.jinja`.
-#}

{#-
  value_type(column) — the Rust type of a column's value, nullability aside.

  The opinions, stated here once rather than in every branch:

  * An integer becomes the Rust integer of the same width, signed as the column
    is. `isize` and `usize` are not used: their width is the platform's, and a
    column's is the server's.
  * BIT becomes `u64`. sqlx decodes a BIT column into any unsigned integer, and
    MariaDB's BIT is at most 64 bits wide, so `u64` holds every declaration.
  * DECIMAL becomes `super::Decimal`, an adapter over the exact decimal text
    the server sends. It is the only lossless mapping that costs no dependency:
    `f64` cannot hold `decimal(38,10)`, and sqlx refuses to decode a DECIMAL
    into `String`. A project that wants arithmetic replaces this one branch
    with `rust_decimal::Decimal` or `bigdecimal::BigDecimal` and turns on the
    matching sqlx feature — which is the whole reason the mapping lives here
    and not in `tpl`.
  * DATE, DATETIME and TIMESTAMP become the chrono types sqlx documents for
    them, reached through sqlx's own re-export so that the crate needs no
    direct chrono dependency. TIMESTAMP is the only one of the three that
    decodes into `DateTime<Utc>`, and DATETIME the only one that decodes into
    `NaiveDateTime`; both were measured.
  * TIME becomes `MySqlTime`, sqlx's own type. A MariaDB TIME is a signed
    interval of up to ±838:59:59, which is not a time of day: `NaiveTime`
    decodes the fixture's value and errors on any value outside a day.
  * YEAR becomes `u16`, which is what sqlx accepts for the type.
  * INET4 and INET6 become `std::net::Ipv4Addr` and `std::net::Ipv6Addr`. The
    two arrive as character data and sqlx parses them, which makes the standard
    library's own address types available at no cost.
  * UUID becomes `String`. MariaDB's native UUID arrives as its 36-character
    text, and sqlx's `Uuid` decodes 16 raw bytes, so the text is what this
    server actually hands a caller.
  * The eight spatial types become `super::Wkb`, an adapter over the bytes the
    server sends: a four-byte SRID followed by well-known binary. sqlx decodes
    a GEOMETRY column into nothing at all, which is what the adapter is for.
  * A JSON column is not a branch of its own because the model never reports
    one: MariaDB reports such a column as `longtext`, per FR-CAT-038.
-#}
{% macro value_type(column) -%}
{%- set kind = column | sql_type -%}
{%- if kind == "tinyint" -%}{{ "u8" if column.unsigned else "i8" }}
{%- elif kind == "smallint" -%}{{ "u16" if column.unsigned else "i16" }}
{%- elif kind == "mediumint" -%}{{ "u32" if column.unsigned else "i32" }}
{%- elif kind == "int" -%}{{ "u32" if column.unsigned else "i32" }}
{%- elif kind == "bigint" -%}{{ "u64" if column.unsigned else "i64" }}
{%- elif kind == "bit" -%}u64
{%- elif kind == "decimal" -%}super::Decimal
{%- elif kind == "float" -%}f32
{%- elif kind == "double" -%}f64
{%- elif kind == "char" -%}String
{%- elif kind == "varchar" -%}String
{%- elif kind == "tinytext" -%}String
{%- elif kind == "text" -%}String
{%- elif kind == "mediumtext" -%}String
{%- elif kind == "longtext" -%}String
{%- elif kind == "enum" -%}String
{%- elif kind == "set" -%}String
{%- elif kind == "uuid" -%}String
{%- elif kind == "binary" -%}Vec<u8>
{%- elif kind == "varbinary" -%}Vec<u8>
{%- elif kind == "tinyblob" -%}Vec<u8>
{%- elif kind == "blob" -%}Vec<u8>
{%- elif kind == "mediumblob" -%}Vec<u8>
{%- elif kind == "longblob" -%}Vec<u8>
{%- elif kind == "date" -%}sqlx::types::chrono::NaiveDate
{%- elif kind == "datetime" -%}sqlx::types::chrono::NaiveDateTime
{%- elif kind == "timestamp" -%}sqlx::types::chrono::DateTime<sqlx::types::chrono::Utc>
{%- elif kind == "time" -%}sqlx::mysql::types::MySqlTime
{%- elif kind == "year" -%}u16
{%- elif kind == "inet4" -%}std::net::Ipv4Addr
{%- elif kind == "inet6" -%}std::net::Ipv6Addr
{%- elif kind == "geometry" -%}super::Wkb
{%- elif kind == "geometrycollection" -%}super::Wkb
{%- elif kind == "linestring" -%}super::Wkb
{%- elif kind == "multilinestring" -%}super::Wkb
{%- elif kind == "multipoint" -%}super::Wkb
{%- elif kind == "multipolygon" -%}super::Wkb
{%- elif kind == "point" -%}super::Wkb
{%- elif kind == "polygon" -%}super::Wkb
{%- else -%}
{{- fail("rust/_types.jinja maps no Rust type onto the column " ~ column.table_name ~ "." ~ column.name ~ ", whose column_type is " ~ column.column_type ~ ". FR-EX-008 obliges this macro to cover every data_type the three schemas report: add a branch for it rather than letting an unmapped type reach the Rust compiler as a guess.") -}}
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro %}

{#-
  field_type(column) — the Rust type of a struct field, nullability honoured.

  A nullable column is not the same Rust type as a non-nullable one, and
  `Option<T>` is where the language puts the difference: sqlx decodes a NULL
  into `None` without erroring and refuses to decode one into `T`, so the
  compiler makes a caller answer the question before it reads the value.
-#}
{% macro field_type(column) -%}
{%- if column is nullable -%}Option<{{ value_type(column) }}>
{%- else -%}{{ value_type(column) }}
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro %}

{#-
  filter_type(column) — the Rust type of one field of a search filter.

  Always `Option<T>` over the value type, whether the column is nullable or
  not: `None` means the field contributes no condition. Nullability is asked
  separately, through the `…_is_null` field a nullable column also gets.
-#}
{% macro filter_type(column) -%}
Option<{{ value_type(column) }}>
{%- endmacro %}

{#-
  is_heap(column) — does the column's Rust type own a heap allocation?

  Four of the types this mapping produces are not `Copy` — `String`, `Vec<u8>`
  and the two adapters — and every one of the other thirty-five is. The
  distinction is not cosmetic: the compile gate refuses a `.clone()` on a
  `Copy` type as `clippy::clone_on_copy`, and it refuses a `&` on one passed to
  a generic parameter as `clippy::needless_borrows_for_generic_args`. Both
  macros below ask this question rather than restating its answer.

  It was measured, not assumed: clippy named every field of the rendered crate
  it wanted unborrowed, and the set is exactly the complement of the four.
-#}
{% macro is_heap(column) -%}
{%- if value_type(column) in ["String", "Vec<u8>", "super::Decimal", "super::Wkb"] -%}yes
{%- else -%}no
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro %}

{#-
  owned(expression, column) — read a field into an owned value.
-#}
{% macro owned(expression, column) -%}
{%- set heap = is_heap(column) -%}
{%- if "yes" in heap -%}{{ expression }}.clone()
{%- else -%}{{ expression }}
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro %}

{#-
  bind(expression, column) — read a field into a query parameter.

  A `Copy` value is passed by value; anything else is borrowed, because moving
  it out of the row or the key the caller lent would not compile.
-#}
{% macro bind(expression, column) -%}
{%- set heap = is_heap(column) -%}
{%- if "yes" in heap -%}&{{ expression }}
{%- else -%}{{ expression }}
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro %}

{#-
  sanitised(name) — a catalogue name with the punctuation Rust refuses in an
  identifier replaced by an underscore.

  This is needed because FR-ENV-031 passes a character outside `A-Za-z` through
  the naming filters unchanged: `pascal` renders the column
  `freight.legacy_edi_field.back`tick` as `Back`tick`, which is not an
  identifier in any language. The underscore is chosen rather than removal
  because `pascal` then treats the position as a word boundary, per
  FR-ENV-030, and the column arrives as `BackTick`.

  The set below is the ASCII punctuation a quoted MariaDB identifier may carry.
  It is closed and it is not complete: a name beginning with a digit, or one
  whose sanitised form collides with another column's, still renders an
  identifier the Rust compiler refuses. That is deliberate — the compile gate
  of FR-EX-009 is the reader that reports it, and it reports it before the code
  is committed rather than after. A character outside ASCII is left alone: Rust
  admits it in an identifier, and `freight.legacy_edi_field.posição` is the
  column that proves it.
-#}
{% macro sanitised(name) -%}
{{- name | replace("`", "_") | replace('"', "_") | replace("'", "_")
         | replace("\\", "_") | replace("/", "_") | replace(".", "_")
         | replace(",", "_") | replace(";", "_") | replace(":", "_")
         | replace("?", "_") | replace("!", "_") | replace("*", "_")
         | replace("+", "_") | replace("=", "_") | replace("<", "_")
         | replace(">", "_") | replace("(", "_") | replace(")", "_")
         | replace("[", "_") | replace("]", "_") | replace("{", "_")
         | replace("}", "_") | replace("|", "_") | replace("^", "_")
         | replace("~", "_") | replace("%", "_") | replace("&", "_")
         | replace("#", "_") | replace("@", "_") | replace("$", "_")
         | replace("-", "_") | replace(" ", "_") -}}
{%- endmacro %}

{#-
  ident(name) — the lower-case Rust identifier for a catalogue name: a struct
  field, a module, a variable.

  A name whose snake case collides with a Rust keyword is written as a raw
  identifier, `r#match`, which is a legal identifier for every keyword but
  four. `crate`, `self`, `Self` and `super` cannot be raw, per the Reference,
  so those four take a trailing underscore instead.

  The list is the strict and reserved keywords of the 2024 edition. No column
  of the three schemas needs it — `select` is a keyword of Go and of SQL but
  not of Rust — so the escape is a guard rather than a demonstration, and the
  compile gate is what would report its absence.
-#}
{% macro ident(name) -%}
{%- set base = sanitised(name) | snake -%}
{%- if base in ["crate", "self", "Self", "super"] -%}{{ base }}_
{%- elif base in ["as", "async", "await", "break", "const", "continue", "dyn",
                  "else", "enum", "extern", "false", "fn", "for", "gen", "if",
                  "impl", "in", "let", "loop", "match", "mod", "move", "mut",
                  "pub", "ref", "return", "static", "struct", "trait", "true",
                  "type", "union", "unsafe", "use", "where", "while",
                  "abstract", "become", "box", "do", "final", "macro",
                  "override", "priv", "try", "typeof", "unsized", "virtual",
                  "yield"] -%}r#{{ base }}
{%- else -%}{{ base }}
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro %}

{#-
  type_name(name) — the Rust type name for a catalogue name.

  FR-ENV-033 records what `pascal` costs: an acronym loses its case, so
  `actor_id` becomes `ActorId` and not `ActorID`. The cost is accepted here
  rather than bought back with an initialism list, which would be a second
  opinion to maintain and a new way for two columns to collide on one field
  name.

  `Self` is the one Rust keyword a PascalCase name can collide with, and it
  cannot be a raw identifier, so it takes a trailing underscore.
-#}
{% macro type_name(name) -%}
{%- set base = sanitised(name) | pascal -%}
{%- if base == "Self" -%}Self_
{%- else -%}{{ base }}
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro %}
